Specification UserInterface

Description The windows the program is worked in: a list of accounts, the
Description register of the account selected in it, and an optional second pane
Description that shows either another account or an import waiting to be reviewed.
Details
Details The shape is three regions. On the left, every account, grouped by the
Details kind of thing it is. On the right, the register of whichever account is
Details selected. The right may be split in two, so that two accounts can be
Details read at once -- or so that an import can be looked over beside the
Details register it is about to change.
Details
Details What is specified here is what the windows show and what selecting
Details things does. Pixels, fonts and column widths are not: those are not
Details worth agreeing in a table, and changing them should not fail a test.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The account list
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DataType AccountGroup
Description The headings the account list is divided into. One per kind of real
Description account, and the categories are not in the list at all: they are
Description reached through a report, not browsed.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value       | Notes                                      |
| Banking     | Bank and Cash accounts                     |
| CreditCards | What is owed on a card                     |
| Investments | Holdings and their cash                    |
| Assets      | A house, a car, anything else owned        |
| Liabilities | A mortgage or a loan                       |

$WIP
DataType AccountSection
Description The two halves the account list is divided into, which are the two
Description halves of a balance sheet: what is owned and what is owed.
Details A section heading carries the total of everything under it, so the two
Details of them are the figures net worth is the difference of. See the balance
Details sheet in Reports.
Examples: EnumerationValues
| Value       | Notes                                       |
| Assets      | What is owned                               |
| Liabilities | What is owed                                |

$WIP
BusinessRule Which section a group is shown under
Description A card is money owed, so it belongs with the mortgage and not with
Description the current account. The list said otherwise until now: the five
Description groups sat in a row with nothing above them, and a card read as
Description though it were a kind of bank account.
Details Banking and Investments and anything else owned are under Assets;
Details CreditCards and anything else owed are under Liabilities.
Details
Details Two of the groups have no heading of their own. The Assets group is a
Details house or a car -- anything owned that is not a bank account or a
Details holding -- and its accounts sit directly beneath the Assets section
Details heading, because a heading called Assets under a heading called Assets
Details would say nothing. The Liabilities group is the same under Liabilities.
Details That is why the indent of a house and the indent of the Banking heading
Details are the same: both are one in from their section.
Examples: SectionOfGroup
| AccountGroup | AccountSection | HasHeadingOfItsOwn | Notes                     |
| Banking      | Assets         | true               | Current and savings       |
| Investments  | Assets         | true               | Holdings and their cash   |
| Assets       | Assets         | false              | A house, a car            |
| CreditCards  | Liabilities    | true               | What is owed on a card    |
| Liabilities  | Liabilities    | false              | A mortgage or a loan      |

Attributes SectionOfGroup
| Attribute          | Type           | Default |
| AccountGroup       | AccountGroup   | Banking |
| AccountSection     | AccountSection | Assets  |
| HasHeadingOfItsOwn | Boolean        | true    |
| Notes              | String         | none    |

BusinessRule Which group an account is listed under
Description Taken from the account type, so nothing has to be filed by hand.
Details The nominal types have no group: Income, Expense and Equity are
Details categories and the account list does not show them.
Examples: GroupOfType
| AccountType | AccountGroup | Listed |
| Bank        | Banking      | true   |
| Cash        | Banking      | true   |
| CreditCard  | CreditCards  | true   |
| Investment  | Investments  | true   |
| Asset       | Assets       | true   |
| Liability   | Liabilities  | true   |
| Income      | Banking      | false  |
| Expense     | Banking      | false  |
| Equity      | Banking      | false  |

Attributes GroupOfType
| Attribute    | Type         | Default |
| AccountType  | AccountType  | Bank    |
| AccountGroup | AccountGroup | Banking |
| Listed       | Boolean      | true    |

Attributes AccountListRow
Description One line of the account list.
Details A section heading names its section and no group and no account, and
Details carries the total of everything under it. A group heading names both
Details its section and its group, and carries the total of the accounts under
Details it. An account names its section and no group.
Details
Details Indent is the depth on the page: a section heading is 0, a group heading
Details and an account that sits directly under a section are 1, an account
Details under a group heading is 2, and a child of that account is 3.
| Attribute | Type           | Default |
| Section   | AccountSection | Assets  |
| Group     | Text           | none    |
| Account   | AccountPath    | none    |
| Name      | Text           | none    |
| Indent    | Integer        | 0       |
| Balance   | Money          | 0.00    |
| IsHeading | Boolean        | false   |

