| 1    | Register | Assets:Checking | false  |
| 2    | Register | Assets:Checking | true   |

Scenario A review of an import into an account that is not selected names it
Description The import says which account it is for, and that is what the
Description review shows regardless of what was selected, because the import is
Description about that account and not about where the reader happened to be.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are as previous
When account selected : AccountSelect
| Path           |
| Assets:Savings |
When import reviewed : ImportReviewRow
| Line | Date       | Payee  | Account          | Category          | Amount | Disposition |
| 1    | 2026-02-02 | Amazon | Liabilities:Visa | Expenses:Shopping | -31.00 | New         |
Then panes are : PaneState
| Pane | Showing      | Account          | Active |
| 1    | Register     | Assets:Savings   | false  |
| 2    | ImportReview | Liabilities:Visa | true   |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What accepting an import keeps
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description An accepted row keeps what the file called it.
Description
Description A downloaded row arrives with the name the file gave it -- a FITID
Description from a QFX, whatever the bank's CSV uses for a reference -- and that
Description name is the whole reason a second download of the same statement
Description does nothing. So it has to reach the posting that is stored.
Details The review shows a row as date, payee, account, category and amount,
Details because that is what a person reads. Building the transaction back out
Details of those five columns would lose the identifier, and the loss would be
Details silent: the import would look right, the register would look right,
Details and next month's download of the same account would offer every
Details transaction in it again as new.
Details
Details So what the import prepared is what is stored. The columns on the
Details screen are a view of it, not the source of it.
Details
Details The raw name travels the same way and for a related reason: a rule
Details written next year has to be able to match what the bank sent this
Details year, so what the bank sent is kept rather than only what it was
Details turned into. The memo, the check number and whether the bank has
Details settled the row travel with it for no deeper reason than that the file
Details said so and the review is not the place to lose it.
Details
Details Whether a row arrives settled is the import's to say, not the review's:
Details an OFX file's rows are a statement and are cleared, and a CSV says
Details nothing about what the bank has settled. So the review carries what it
Details was given rather than deciding.

Attributes AcceptedPosting
Description One side of a transaction that accepting the review has just added,
Description with the identifier it was imported under. Only the sides of the
Description accepted rows are listed; what the book already held is not.
| Attribute  | Type          | Default   |
| Account    | AccountPath   |           |
| Amount     | Money         | 0.00      |
| Identifier | FitId         | none      |
| Source     | ImportSource  | Ofx       |
| RawName    | Text          | none      |
| Memo       | Text          | none      |
| CheckNo    | CheckNumber   | none      |
| Cleared    | ClearedStatus | Uncleared |

Scenario Accepting an import keeps the identifier the file gave each row
Description The identifier belongs to the side the statement is for. The bank
Description has never heard of the category, so that side carries none.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are as previous
When import reviewed : ImportReviewRow
| Line | Date       | Payee         | Account         | Category           | Amount | Disposition | Identifier  | Source | RawName                      | Memo          | CheckNo |
| 1    | 2026-02-02 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking | Expenses:Groceries | -52.10 | New         | 20260202001 | Ofx    | HARRIS~TEETER~#231~DURHAM~NC | Weekly~shop   | none    |
When import accepted
Then accepted postings are : AcceptedPosting
| Account            | Amount | Identifier  | Source | RawName                      | Memo        | CheckNo | Cleared   |
| Assets:Checking    | -52.10 | 20260202001 | Ofx    | HARRIS~TEETER~#231~DURHAM~NC | Weekly~shop | none    | Cleared   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 52.10  | none        | Ofx    | HARRIS~TEETER~#231~DURHAM~NC | Weekly~shop | none    | Uncleared |

Scenario A CSV row keeps its reference under its own source
Description Two files describing one transaction do not agree on what to call
Description it, so the source travels with the identifier. A reference kept
Description under Csv does not make a later QFX of the same account do nothing,
Description and that is deliberate -- see the identifier scope rule.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are as previous
When import reviewed : ImportReviewRow
| Line | Date       | Payee  | Account         | Category          | Amount | Disposition | Identifier | Source | RawName       |
| 1    | 2026-02-04 | Amazon | Assets:Checking | Expenses:Shopping | -31.00 | New         | REF-99812  | Csv    | AMAZON~MKTPLACE |
When import accepted
Then accepted postings are : AcceptedPosting
| Account           | Amount | Identifier | Source | RawName         | Cleared   |
| Assets:Checking   | -31.00 | REF-99812  | Csv    | AMAZON~MKTPLACE | Cleared   |
| Expenses:Shopping | 31.00  | none       | Csv    | AMAZON~MKTPLACE | Uncleared |

Scenario A row the file gave no identifier is still accepted
Description A QIF carries no identifier, and a row read from one is still worth
Description bringing in. It is the weaker tests -- the date, the amount, the
Description check number -- that keep a second reading of the same QIF from
Description duplicating it.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are as previous
When import reviewed : ImportReviewRow
| Line | Date       | Payee        | Account         | Category           | Amount | Disposition | Identifier | Source | RawName |
| 1    | 2026-02-05 | Corner Store | Assets:Checking | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | New         | none       | Qif    | none    |
When import accepted
Then accepted postings are : AcceptedPosting
| Account            | Amount | Identifier | Source | RawName | Cleared   |
| Assets:Checking    | -12.00 | none       | Qif    | none    | Cleared   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 12.00  | none       | Qif    | none    | Uncleared |

Scenario An unticked row contributes no posting at all
Description The identifier of a row that was not accepted must not be stored
Description either, or the row could never be imported again.
Given the chart of accounts is as previous
Given postings are as previous
When import reviewed : ImportReviewRow
| Line | Date       | Payee         | Account         | Category           | Amount | Disposition | Identifier  | Source | RawName        |
| 1    | 2026-02-02 | Harris Teeter | Assets:Checking | Expenses:Groceries | -52.10 | New         | 20260202001 | Ofx    | HARRIS~TEETER  |
| 2    | 2026-02-03 | Unknown Shop  | Assets:Checking | Expenses:Shopping  | -23.75 | New         | 20260203002 | Ofx    | UNKNOWN~SHOP   |
When review row unticked : ReviewRowSelect
| Line |
| 2    |
When import accepted
Then accepted postings are : AcceptedPosting
| Account            | Amount | Identifier  | Source | RawName       | Cleared   |
| Assets:Checking    | -52.10 | 20260202001 | Ofx    | HARRIS~TEETER | Cleared   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 52.10  | none        | Ofx    | HARRIS~TEETER | Uncleared |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Choosing lines in a register and acting on them
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description A register is where the work gets done, so what can be done to a
Description line is reached from the line itself.
Details A selection is a set of lines. How it was made is the table's business
Details and not the program's: a click takes one, shift extends the run from
Details the line last clicked, and control adds or removes a single line -- so
Details a run and a scattered set are the same thing once they exist, and
Details nothing below needs to know which gesture was used.
Details
Details Everything on the menu acts on the whole selection. Recategorising
Details twenty lines at once is the reason the selection is worth having: a
Details statement arrives with twenty charges from one shop, and categorising
Details them one at a time is the work the program is supposed to save.

Attributes RegisterSelect
Description The lines of the register that are selected, counting from one in
Description the order the register is showing.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Line      | Integer | 1       |

Attributes MenuItem
Description One item of the menu offered on a selection, in the order shown.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Item      | Text    |         |
| Enabled   | Boolean | true    |

Attributes CategoryChoice
| Attribute | Type        | Default |
| Category  | AccountPath |         |

Attributes Recategorised
Description What came of it, counted, because a selection of twenty is too many
Description to check by eye.
| Attribute | Type    | Default |
| Changed   | Integer | 0       |
| Refused   | Integer | 0       |
| Reason    | String  | none    |

$WIP
Scenario The menu on a selection offers what can be done to it
